	abstract = {Sampling generally concerns how a sample of units is selected from a population, while experiments deal with the effects of a treatment or exposure on units and are concerned with the assignment of treatments to units. Real studies typically involve elements of both, with varying control by investigators over sample selection and treatment assignment aspects. Design and model based approaches in sampling and experiments, and in particular studies which combine both elements, are examined in this article. Within a model based approach design based methods can be used based on a conditioning argument which is necessarily somewhat more complex in the case of experimental studies than in studies involving only sampling. Copyright © 2002 John Wiley \& Sons, Ltd.},
